{"name":"Jellyfish Blue","hex":"#95cad0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"jellyfish","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An almost transparent aquatic animal; any one of the acalephs, especially one of the larger species, having a jellylike appearance.","Any of various species of cnidarians in the subphylum Medusozoa, including box jellyfish (class Cubozoa), true jellyfish (class Scyphozoa), stalked jellyfish (class Staurozoa), and certain hydrozoans.\n The medusa phase of these animals rather than a younger life stage."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/jellyfish"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Jellyfish","blocks":[{"text":"Jellyfish, also known as sea jellies or simply jellies, are the medusa-phase of certain gelatinous members of the subphylum Medusozoa, which is a major part of the phylum Cnidaria. Jellyfish are mainly free-swimming marine animals, although a few are anchored to the seabed by stalks rather than being motile. They are made of an umbrella-shaped main body made of mesoglea, known as the bell, and a collection of trailing tentacles on the underside."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Jelly_cc11.jpg/330px-Jelly_cc11.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jellyfish","wikidata":"Q30178"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Sea_Nettle_Jelly_1.jpg/500px-Sea_Nettle_Jelly_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Sea_Nettle_Jelly_1.jpg/1280px-Sea_Nettle_Jelly_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Sea_Nettle_Jelly_1.jpg/500px-Sea_Nettle_Jelly_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A purple-striped jellyfish at the Monterey Bay Aquarium","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASea_Nettle_Jelly_1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/LightRefractsOf_comb-rows_of_ctenophore_Mertensia_ovum.jpg/500px-LightRefractsOf_comb-rows_of_ctenophore_Mertensia_ovum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/LightRefractsOf_comb-rows_of_ctenophore_Mertensia_ovum.jpg/500px-LightRefractsOf_comb-rows_of_ctenophore_Mertensia_ovum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://e